-- ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 0038 — a name is letters, and two names that look the same are the same name (S-03 for good, phase 1b)
--   0036/0037 removed or replaced the characters known to fake a name (a list of invisible characters and 52 dot look-alikes).
--   Every review found more that passed (U+FFFC, U+11174, U+1F797, U+111D0, ∘, ▪, Cyrillic letters), so a member could look
--   exactly like another one and receive the messages meant for them. The list is replaced by two rules, generated from the
--   Unicode data by tools/names/name_rule.mjs and identical in the live app (web/src/live/adapter.js nmNorm/nmProblem/nmSkel):
--   1. ALLOWLIST — what a name may contain (person_name_problem): letters of the Latin, Arabic, Greek or Cyrillic script whose
--      UTS #39 Identifier_Status is Allowed, combining marks on a letter (at most 3), the space, and . - ' ’ ( ) ,
--      Each word is one script (its marks of that script or generic); Latin and Arabic words may mix, Greek or Cyrillic only as
--      the script of the whole name. No digits, symbols, other punctuation, private-use or unassigned characters; every word has
--      a letter. Names are stored normalised (person_name_norm: invisible characters and the tatweel removed, NFKC, one space).
--      The browser (anon/authenticated) cannot store a name that breaks it — the write is refused (23514) with the sentence the
--      app shows (EN, translated to Arabic by the app); sign-up (handle_new_user) refuses it for everyone.
--   2. SKELETON — profiles.name_key = person_name_key(name): the TR39 confusable skeleton (confusables.txt 16.0.0), lower case,
--      Arabic short vowels dropped. Two members with the same key are "the same name": the ✎ New message picker, the resolver
--      (it fetches every member with the key before sending) and the chat header tell them apart.
--   3. EXISTING NAMES — the invisible characters and U+FFFC (an object-replacement box that draws nothing) are removed, which
--      changes nothing a person reads; a name that is then allowed is kept as it reads. Any other name is kept, never renamed
--      into someone else, and flagged: profiles.name_needs_fix — the member is asked to update it on their own profile, the
--      picker always shows what tells that member apart with a warning and never sends to the bare name. A server write (SQL,
--      service role, seeds) of a name that breaks the rule is kept and flagged the same way.
--   4. my_conversations() also returns name_needs_fix (the chat list and header warn about such a name); open_candidates()
--      lists the two new columns (it "returns setof profiles").
-- The picker's separator "·" can no longer be part of a name (it is not a letter). Every new object has its own GRANT/REVOKE
-- (0026). Safe to run twice.
-- ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

alter table public.profiles add column if not exists name_key text;
alter table public.profiles add column if not exists name_needs_fix boolean not null default false;

-- ── 1, 2. the rule (generated: tools/names/name_rule.mjs --write) ────
-- >>> generated by tools/names/name_rule.mjs from Unicode 16.0.0 (UTS #39 data: tools/names/unicode-16.0.0) — do not edit by hand
